{"url": "https://www.stonybrook.edu/commcms/chemistry/Education/Rotations%20FAQ.php?accordion=content-d19e132", "final_url": "https://www.stonybrook.edu/commcms/chemistry/Education/Rotations%20FAQ.php?accordion=content-d19e132", "status": 200, "method": "http", "fetched": "2026-10-24", "text": "Rotations FAQ | Department of Chemistry\nSkip Navigation\nSearch Text\nSelect Search Scope\nSearch This Site\nJust This Site\nSearch SBU Website\nSBU Website\nSearch\nApply to our Graduate Program!\nIntranet\nSearch\nSearch Text\nSearch\nSite\nSite\nCollege of Arts & Sciences\nDepartment of Chemistry\nHome\nAbout\nMessage from the Chair\nContact Us\nLeadership\nBirth of MRI\nFacilities\nExternal Advisory Board\nHonoring Leaders in Chemistry\nInterdisciplinary Research Partners\nTraining Programs\nNews\nNewsletters\nCurrent News & Archives\nPhoto Galleries\nCommencement\nChemistry Research Day\nAcademics\nMission Statement\nPhD Program\nHow to Apply\nPhD Handbook\nRotations FAQ\nNIH T32: CBTP\nNSF NRT: QuADS\nMaster's Program\nUndergraduate Program\nUndergraduate Advising\nClass Schedules\nSpring 2026\nFall 2026\nSummer 2026\nResearch\nResearch Areas\nPatents\nEvents\nSeminar Schedules\nNamed Lecture Series\nPUI Lecture Series\nSymposia\nArts Everywhere\nChemistry and P&A 5k\nPeople\nFaculty\nStaff\nAlumni\nStudent Engagement\nDiversity & Inclusion\nOutreach\nGraduate Chemical Society\nUG American Chemical Society\nGiving\nJob Opportunities\nRotations FAQ\nOur rotation program is designed to give PhD students experience in multiple research\nlabs before deciding which lab they would like to do their PhD thesis in. A rotation\nis a short research position within an individual lab. Experience in multiple different\nlabs can aid students in deciding which projects and lab environments best suit their\ninterests.\nProspective students are asked to give preferences for their 1st rotation labs in June. Typically, students are matched up with a rotation advisor\nin July and begin their rotations on the first day of classes (usually late August).\nA second rotation begins 6 weeks after the start of the 1st rotation. A 3rd rotation will start 6 weeks after the 2nd starts (12 weeks after the beginning of the semester).\nPlease note that all rotation assignments need to be finalized though the rotation\nadvisor or graduate program coordinator.\nFAQ:\nWhich labs can I rotate in?\nChemistry PhD students are allowed to rotate with any of the Core or Affiliated Faculty\non our departmental website.\nDo I have to do rotations?\nNo. Although most of our PhD students participate in rotations it is not required.\nNotably, if a student does not complete 2 full rotations they must take 6 graduate\ncourse to meet our degree requirements. Students who complete 2 rotations only need\nto take 5 graduate courses to meet the degree requirements.\nWhy would I choose not to do rotations?\nIf you have already decided on a research advisor and obtained a commitment from them\nthat you can join their lab.\nFacebookTwitteryoutubelinkedinprint\nDepartment of ChemistryStony Brook University100 Nicolls Road, 104 ChemistryStony Brook, NY 11794-3400\nPhone: (631) 632 7880Fax: (631) 632 7960\nUndergraduate program Graduate program\nDiscrimination\nSexual Misconduct\nAccessibility Barrier\n\u00a9Admin Login2024Stony Brook University", "links": [["Skip Navigation", "https://www.stonybrook.edu/commcms/chemistry/Education/Rotations%20FAQ.php?accordion=content-d19e132#main-site-content"], ["Apply to our Graduate Program!", "https://graduateadmissions.stonybrook.edu/apply/"], ["Intranet", "https://www.stonybrook.edu/commcms/chemistry/intranet/Intranet.php"], ["", "https://www.stonybrook.edu/"], ["College of Arts & Sciences", "https://www.stonybrook.edu/commcms/cas"], ["Department of Chemistry", "https://www.stonybrook.edu/commcms/chemistry"], ["Home", "https://www.stonybrook.edu/chemistry/"], ["About", "https://www.stonybrook.edu/commcms/chemistry/Education/Rotations%20FAQ.php?acco